# The MSVC toolset is chosen by the build scripts, not by the harness

Backlog-adjacent: `test/run-tests.bat` used to be the only place that pinned MSVC
14.44 for nvcc (`vcvars64 -vcvars_ver=14.44`) and retried with
`-allow-unsupported-compiler` when the host compiler was rejected. A plain
`build.bat`, or a hand-written `cmake` command, took the newest toolset in the
Visual Studio instance - 14.50 in VS 18 - which CUDA 13 refuses in
`crt/host_config.h`.

What was measured here on 2026-11-26, Windows 11, CUDA 13.0.48, VS 18 BuildTools
holding MSVC 14.44.35207 and 14.50.35717:

## 1. A Visual Studio generator cannot be pinned (so the scripts use Ninja)

    cmake -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -T version=14.44.35207 ...
      CMake Error at CMakeLists.txt:3 (project):
        Generator Visual Studio 18 2026
        given toolset and version specification v145,version=14.44.35207
        contains an invalid version specification.

    cmake -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -T v144,version=14.44.35207 ...
      Failed to run MSBuild command ... to get the value of VCTargetsPath

    cmake -G "Visual Studio 18 2026" -A x64 -DCMAKE_VS_PLATFORM_TOOLSET_VERSION=14.44 ...
      -- The CXX compiler identification is unknown
      CMake Error: No CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER could be found.

The default configure of the same generator reports MSVC 19.50.35726.0, i.e. the
toolset nvcc would reject. `CMakeLists.txt` now refuses that combination at
configure time instead of letting nvcc invite the flag:

CMake Error at CMakeLists.txt:23 (message):
  MSVC 1950 (19.50.35726.0) is newer than CUDA 13 accepts (14.44):
  host_config.h rejects it and nvcc stops at the first .cu, and a Visual
  Studio generator always takes the newest toolset in the instance.  Build
  with build.bat, which pins 14.44 through vcvars64, or configure with Ninja
  inside `vcvars64.bat -vcvars_ver=14.44`, or pass
  -DOFXPLUGIN_ENABLE_CUDA=OFF for the CPU path.



## 2. build.bat pins 14.44 itself

    templates\minimal-cuda-ofx\build.bat Release

[build] generator: Ninja (no NVIDIA build customisations for the installed Visual Studio)
Microsoft (R) C/C++-Optimierungscompiler Version 19.44.35224 f�r x64
Bundle: Z:\Tools-DEV\OFXPluginDeveloper_Local\templates\minimal-cuda-ofx\build\bundle\MinimalCudaGain.ofx.bundle

Exit status 0. The `MSVC_PIN` the script found is `14.44.35207`; it is passed to
`vcvars64 -vcvars_ver` before CMake runs, and the cl it lands on is 19.44.35224.

## 3. test\run-tests.bat uses the same pin and no longer retries with the flag

    templates\minimal-cuda-ofx\test\run-tests.bat Release nobuild

== [1/3] artifact (exports, dependents, device code) ==
Microsoft (R) C/C++-Optimierungscompiler Version 19.44.35224 f�r x64
== [2/3] GPU harness (real device code, not emulated) ==
== [3/3] host contract (LoadLibraryExW + fake host) ==
contract_test: 162 checks, 0 failed
contract_test: 162 checks, 0 failed
contract_test: 162 checks, 0 failed
all Windows checks passed

Exit status 0, "all Windows checks passed", and no `-allow-unsupported-compiler`
line in the output - the harness compiled the `.cu` with the pinned toolset on the
first attempt.

## What is not proven here

The Visual Studio generator branch that refuses a too-new toolset
(`VS_CUDA_CUSTOMISATIONS` present, `CMAKE_GENERATOR` forced) did not run: this
machine has no NVIDIA MSBuild customisations for VS 18, so build.bat takes the
Ninja branch and the earlier "no customisations" error fires first. The reasoning
is in the script; the measurable part - that the generator takes 14.50 - is item 1.
